  "chapter": "Chapter 3. Insurance and Indemnification",
  "section": "3.5. Commercial General Liability (CGL)",
  "subsection": "E. Commercial General Liability (CGL) Insurance",
  "text": "(b) [3:390] Applicable to post-termination torts? Courts disagree on whether an exclusion for “employment-related practices” applies to defamation or other torts committed by the insured employer after termination of employment: 1) [3:391] Narrow interpretation: According to one view, post-termination employer torts are “employment related” only if the tort's relationship to the employment is direct and proximate. I.e., a defamatory remark must have been part of the termination or directly and proximately resulted therefrom: “Any broader interpretation … would defeat the reasonable expectation of the insured.” [HS Services, Inc. v. Nationwide Mut. Ins. Co. (9th Cir. 1997) 109 F3d 642, 647 (applying Calif. law); Golden Eagle Ins. Corp. v.",
